1
00:00:01,417 --> 00:00:03,458
SponsorScout. A final project pitch by Akshit

2
00:00:03,458 --> 00:00:05,583
Verma. I'll show what already ran,

3
00:00:05,583 --> 00:00:07,042
and what I will build.

4
00:00:07,042 --> 00:00:09,375
The question isn't which companies to apply

5
00:00:09,375 --> 00:00:12,000
to. It's which ones to network with.

6
00:00:12,833 --> 00:00:14,625
SponsorScout will be a Claude Code workflow

7
00:00:14,625 --> 00:00:16,750
that tells an international student,

8
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:18,125
for each company,

9
00:00:18,125 --> 00:00:19,750
whether to apply,

10
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:21,458
network, check by hand,

11
00:00:21,458 --> 00:00:24,125
or skip, with the record behind every

12
00:00:24,125 --> 00:00:24,791
answer.

13
00:00:24,791 --> 00:00:26,375
Done, it will be a repository,

14
00:00:26,375 --> 00:00:28,958
in Python and Claude Code on public

15
00:00:28,958 --> 00:00:31,075
data: a recipe,

16
00:00:31,166 --> 00:00:32,958
a prototype, tests,

17
00:00:32,958 --> 00:00:34,375
an evaluation log,

18
00:00:34,375 --> 00:00:36,041
and a report you can trace row

19
00:00:36,041 --> 00:00:36,958
by row.

20
00:00:36,958 --> 00:00:39,000
Each company gets three checks.

21
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:40,750
Has it sponsored data roles,

22
00:00:40,750 --> 00:00:43,283
in public H one B records?

23
00:00:43,291 --> 00:00:45,166
Has it raised money recently,

24
00:00:45,166 --> 00:00:48,500
in S E C Form D filings?

25
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:50,958
Is an entry-level data job open on

26
00:00:50,958 --> 00:00:52,125
its board today?

27
00:00:52,125 --> 00:00:53,625
The engine's own scorer,

28
00:00:53,625 --> 00:00:55,500
which I had Claude Code run on

29
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:58,208
October third, gives a sponsor with no

30
00:00:58,208 --> 00:00:59,625
open job a zero,

31
00:00:59,625 --> 00:01:00,950
and skips it.

32
00:01:01,208 --> 00:01:03,875
SponsorScout puts it on a networking list.

33
00:01:03,875 --> 00:01:06,000
So a student can stop applying where

34
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:06,958
nothing is open,

35
00:01:06,958 --> 00:01:09,791
and keep the sponsors worth contacting.

36
00:01:09,791 --> 00:01:12,241
I also looked at My Visa Jobs.

37
00:01:12,333 --> 00:01:14,500
It lists sponsors and their past job

38
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:17,041
counts. It doesn't take a sponsor with

39
00:01:17,041 --> 00:01:19,208
nothing open and tell you to network

40
00:01:19,208 --> 00:01:19,808
there.

41
00:01:19,708 --> 00:01:22,416
I'm targeting data analyst and data engineer

42
00:01:22,416 --> 00:01:24,791
roles, so my reader is a data

43
00:01:24,791 --> 00:01:28,166
hiring manager. In sixty seconds,

44
00:01:28,166 --> 00:01:30,533
they'll run one offline command,

45
00:01:30,708 --> 00:01:31,908
open the report,

46
00:01:31,916 --> 00:01:33,708
and trace a networking row to its

47
00:01:33,708 --> 00:01:34,416
source.

48
00:01:34,416 --> 00:01:35,866
This already ran.

49
00:01:36,083 --> 00:01:37,250
On October third,

50
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:39,708
the prototype fetched sixteen job boards

51
00:01:39,708 --> 00:01:42,583
and sorted one hundred twenty-three sponsors:

52
00:01:42,583 --> 00:01:43,875
two to apply,

53
00:01:43,875 --> 00:01:45,041
one to network,

54
00:01:45,041 --> 00:01:47,458
one hundred seven to check by hand,

55
00:01:47,458 --> 00:01:48,866
thirteen to skip.

56
00:01:49,041 --> 00:01:50,833
The job seeker is fictional.

57
00:01:50,833 --> 00:01:52,575
Run one got one wrong:

58
00:01:52,583 --> 00:01:55,000
Zoox landed in apply on part-time,

59
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,408
contract student-worker postings.

60
00:01:57,541 --> 00:01:58,791
Claude flagged it.

61
00:01:58,791 --> 00:01:59,750
I chose the fix,

62
00:01:59,750 --> 00:02:01,575
and a test pins it.

63
00:02:01,666 --> 00:02:04,375
All twenty-five tests passed again on October

64
00:02:04,375 --> 00:02:04,975
twelfth.

65
00:02:05,166 --> 00:02:06,991
What can a reviewer conclude?

66
00:02:07,249 --> 00:02:09,708
Source grounding: every number is labelled

67
00:02:09,708 --> 00:02:12,116
a record or my own input.

68
00:02:12,333 --> 00:02:15,333
Verification: an unconfirmed board never

69
00:02:15,333 --> 00:02:16,499
reaches the scorer,

70
00:02:16,499 --> 00:02:18,124
which would treat it as open.

71
00:02:18,124 --> 00:02:20,791
Agent loops: Claude Code stops at the

72
00:02:20,791 --> 00:02:23,041
human gates for my sign-off.

73
00:02:23,041 --> 00:02:25,157
Evaluation is still to come:

74
00:02:25,249 --> 00:02:26,999
a hand-checked set of verdicts,

75
00:02:26,999 --> 00:02:28,791
with the wrong ones logged.

76
00:02:28,791 --> 00:02:29,791
It will never apply,

77
00:02:29,791 --> 00:02:32,407
email, or contact anyone.

78
00:02:32,416 --> 00:02:33,582
The person decides.

79
00:02:33,582 --> 00:02:36,041
And it won't score résumé fit,

80
00:02:36,041 --> 00:02:38,374
so no real résumé goes in.

81
00:02:38,374 --> 00:02:40,907
First, I'll build a board resolver.

82
00:02:41,166 --> 00:02:43,374
Today sixteen of the hundred twenty-three

83
00:02:43,374 --> 00:02:45,824
companies have a confirmed board.

84
00:02:45,916 --> 00:02:47,541
You'll know it ran when that number

85
00:02:47,541 --> 00:02:50,416
rises, each new board saved with its

86
00:02:50,416 --> 00:02:52,332
raw response and a test.

87
00:02:52,332 --> 00:02:53,699
What could sink it:

88
00:02:53,791 --> 00:02:55,416
many of the other hundred and seven

89
00:02:55,416 --> 00:02:57,324
may have no public board,

90
00:02:57,332 --> 00:02:58,824
and I don't know how many.

91
00:02:58,999 --> 00:03:01,366
I'll use only public Greenhouse,

92
00:03:01,499 --> 00:03:03,166
Lever and Ashby boards,

93
00:03:03,166 --> 00:03:05,832
never scrape, and report coverage even if

94
00:03:05,832 --> 00:03:06,582
it's low.

95
00:03:06,582 --> 00:03:08,791
I also don't know if recent funding

96
00:03:08,791 --> 00:03:12,166
predicts hiring. I'll find out by re-checking

97
00:03:12,166 --> 00:03:14,624
the networking targets' boards after fourteen

98
00:03:14,624 --> 00:03:17,999
days, and logging whether a posting appeared.

99
00:03:17,999 --> 00:03:20,166
I picked it because I'm job hunting

100
00:03:20,166 --> 00:03:22,291
too, and I wanted a smarter strategy

101
00:03:22,291 --> 00:03:24,949
than a hundred-plus applications a day.

102
00:03:24,999 --> 00:03:25,949
It's a mix.

103
00:03:26,249 --> 00:03:27,832
What I can show is judgment,

104
00:03:27,832 --> 00:03:30,457
not code: Claude Code wrote most of

105
00:03:30,457 --> 00:03:32,582
the prototype; I set its rules,

106
00:03:32,582 --> 00:03:34,499
like requiring recent funding,

107
00:03:34,499 --> 00:03:36,699
and signed its human gates.

108
00:03:36,916 --> 00:03:38,032
New for me:

109
00:03:38,124 --> 00:03:40,999
evaluation, and resolving boards at scale.

110
00:03:42,374 --> 00:03:44,041
SponsorScout. A pitch by Akshit Verma.

